>>
endobj
154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (In 2024 the top five were led by DKI Jakarta, followed by Kepulauan Bangka Belitung, Jawa Tengah, Kepulauan Riau, and Jambi. In 2025 the ranking shifts to Jawa Barat, DKI Jakarta, Papua Barat, Aceh, and Papua. The scatter plot moves toward higher price pressure in 2025, while DKI Jakarta remains in the top five in both years.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (In 2025 the ranking reshuffles. Jawa Barat moves into first place, with DKI Jakarta in second, followed by Papua Barat, Aceh, and Papua. The scores are also more evenly spread, so the lead of a single province is much smaller than in the previous year.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (Because wage and population values stay the same across both years, these shifts come mainly from the inflation component of the score. In the scatter plot, the price index axis extends further to the right in 2025, which shows that price pressure rose across many provinces and reshaped which ones look attractive. DKI Jakarta is the only province that stays in the top five in both years, which makes it the most consistent performer in the analysis.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 86 0 R
/E (The takeaway for decision makers is that an expansion ranking is not fixed. A province that looks strongest in one year can lose ground when price pressure changes, so this dashboard is best used as a regular monitoring tool rather than a one-time snapshot.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
